# Shabat: definition, example, classical converse and the unquantified programme.
add(469,"P000862","definition","Two distinct translation parametrizations",
 r"A hypersurface germ \(\Gamma\subset\mathbb C^n\) is written both as \(\gamma_1+\cdots+\gamma_{n-1}\) and as \(\delta_1+\cdots+\delta_{n-1}\); permutations and translations are excluded.",
 r"A translation parametrization of a smooth hypersurface germ is a local biholomorphism \((t_1,\ldots,t_{n-1})\mapsto\sum_i\gamma_i(t_i)\) onto the germ. Distinctness up to permutations and constant translations is a minimal condition; the classical converse needs stronger local nondegeneracy.",
 r"Without the immersion and local uniqueness conditions, arbitrary sums of curve germs can have smaller dimension or singularities.",
 r"The classical subject and its precise genericity conditions are reviewed in \citet[Definitions~2.1--2.2]{little1983translation}.",["little1983translation"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(469,"P000862-P000863","construction","Theta divisors as sums of Abel curves",
 r"For a smooth curve \(X\) of genus \(n\), the notes write \(\Theta=jX+\cdots+jX\), with \(n-1\) summands, and \(\Theta=K-\Theta\).",
 r"Let \(X\) be a smooth projective complex curve of genus \(g\ge2\), \(j:X\to J(X)\) an Abel map based at \(p_0\), and \(W_{g-1}\) the image of effective divisors of degree \(g-1\). Then \(W_{g-1}\) is a translate of the theta divisor and is invariant under \(x\mapsto [K_X-(2g-2)p_0]-x\).",
 r"The symmetry follows from Riemann--Roch: an effective divisor \(D\) of degree \(g-1\) has \(h^0(K_X-D)=h^0(D)\). At regular points, two complementary effective divisors give the two local sums.",
 r"This is preexisting Riemann theta-divisor theory; \citet[Example~4.1]{little1983translation} gives the exact translation-manifold example.",["little1983translation"],literal="correct")
add(469,"P000863","claim","When the two Abel curves are distinct",
 r"\(X\ne-jX\) is said to be equivalent to nonhyperellipticity.",
 r"For a smooth complex curve of genus \(g\ge2\), \(j(X)\) is a translate of \(-j(X)\) if and only if \(X\) is hyperelliptic. In the hyperelliptic case the two theta-divisor translation decompositions arising from inversion coincide.",
 r"The left-hand \(X\) is shorthand for its Abel image, and equality must be considered up to translation. For the converse, an inversion symmetry yields a fixed degree-two divisor class \(p+\iota(p)\), hence a degree-two map to \(\mathbb P^1\).",
 r"Classical Abel-curve geometry; the hyperelliptic exception is explicit in \citet[Sec.~4, following Example~4.1]{little1983translation}.",["little1983translation"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(469,"P000863","theorem","The Lie--Wirtinger converse with nondegeneracy",
 r"All double translation surfaces are said to be theta divisors and their degenerations; the parenthesis claims that a small deformation is a theta-divisor germ.",
 r"Take a smooth non-linear hypersurface germ in \(\mathbb C^n\) with two locally invertible sum parametrizations. Assume their \(2n-2\) tangent lines at the base point are distinct, every generating curve has acceleration transverse to the tangent hyperplane, and varying at least one first-system parameter while fixing the others changes every second-system parameter. Then the germ is a translate of the lift of an Abel-divisor locus in the generalized Jacobian of a reduced, connected canonical curve of degree \(2n-2\) and arithmetic genus \(n\).",
 r"The first two extra assumptions are Little's Definition~2.2; the last is condition (*) in Proposition~3.7. Singular or reducible curves give generalized Jacobians. Smoothability of every possible degeneration is not asserted here.",
 r"Lie--Wirtinger is classical, not a new 1979 assertion. \citet[Corollary~3.5, Proposition~3.7, Theorem~3.9]{little1983translation} supplies a precise later proof and distinguishes the missing conditions. Its Theorem~5.1 recovers smooth nonhyperelliptic Jacobians when the ambient variety is principally polarized.",["little1983translation"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial")
add(469,"P000863","conjecture","A universal explanation of integrability by translation geometry",
 r"Gelfand conjectures that all integrable systems have this theorem as their basis, and that it is the first of a large family of such theorems.",
 r"No class of systems, notion of integrability, construction of an associated hypersurface, or meaning of being a basis is specified. There is no uniquely recoverable quantified conjecture.",
 r"The appearance of theta functions in algebraically integrable systems motivates the comment, but cannot turn a universal informal analogy into a theorem.",
 r"The checked Lie--Wirtinger source concerns translation hypersurfaces, not all integrable systems \citep{little1983translation}. Its theorem therefore supplies no exact publication match for this universal programme.",["little1983translation"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the recorded programme remains underdetermined; it is not labelled an open theorem.",literal="not determinable",match="thematic only",speaker="I. M. Gelfand")

# Høegh-Krohn: keep the construction separate from the dimension-specific results.
add(470,"P000863-P000864","construction","The current group and its one-form Hilbert space",
 r"\(G^X=C^\infty_0(X,G)\), with \(G\) compact simple; the one-form scalar product uses the Riemannian metric and the Killing form.",
 r"For a smooth Riemannian manifold \(X\) and compact semisimple \(G\), let \(\mathcal G=C^\infty_c(X,G)\), meaning maps equal to the identity off a compact set. Complete \(\Omega^1_c(X,\mathfrak g)\) for \(\int_X \langle\omega_1,\omega_2\rangle_{g,B}\,d\mu\), with \(B\) an \(\operatorname{Ad}G\)-invariant positive inner product, for example minus the Killing form.",
 r"The Killing form itself is negative definite on a compact semisimple real Lie algebra. The sign is material to the Hilbert-space construction. The trace can be taken on either tangent or Lie-algebra endomorphisms because \(\operatorname{tr}(AB)=\operatorname{tr}(BA)\).",
 r"This setting predates the lecture in \citet[Secs.~2--3]{albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy}; the general geometric formulation is explicit in \citet[Sec.~6]{wallach1987gauge}.",["albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy","wallach1987gauge"])
add(470,"P000864","construction","The infinite-dimensional Gaussian realization",
 r"The notation is \(L^2(\mathcal H,\mu)\), with a Gaussian analogous to \(e^{-|x|^2/2}dx\), invariant under rotations and quasi-invariant under translations.",
 r"On an infinite-dimensional separable real Hilbert space \(\mathcal H\), covariance \(I\) does not define a countably additive Gaussian Borel probability on \(\mathcal H\). Use an isonormal Gaussian process on a probability space, a nuclear dual containing \(\mathcal H\), or the symmetric Fock space over its complexification. Cameron--Martin translations are the vectors of \(\mathcal H\); abstract second quantization implements every orthogonal operator.",
 r"If independent coordinates had law \(N(0,1)\), their squared sum would diverge almost surely, so a sample would not lie in \(\ell^2\). An arbitrary orthogonal map need not act pointwise on a chosen Banach realization; its Gaussian unitary is nevertheless defined.",
 r"\citet[Secs.~1 and~5]{wallach1987gauge} uses distribution spaces and Gaussian Hilbert spaces; \citet[Sec.~5]{albeverioetal2015brownian} gives the Fock/white-noise construction. The literal Hilbert-space probability notation is false.",["wallach1987gauge","albeverioetal2015brownian"],literal="false as written")
add(470,"P000864","claim","The Maurer--Cartan cocycle and affine action",
 r"\(\alpha(\varphi)\omega=\operatorname{Ad}\varphi\,\omega+\varphi^{-1}d\varphi\) is declared a group homomorphism.",
 r"With \(\pi(\varphi)\omega=\operatorname{Ad}\varphi\,\omega\), the correct left cocycle is \(\beta(\varphi)=d\varphi\,\varphi^{-1}\), since \(\beta(\varphi\psi)=\beta(\varphi)+\pi(\varphi)\beta(\psi)\). Thus \(\alpha(\varphi)\omega=\pi(\varphi)\omega+\beta(\varphi)\) is a left affine action.",
 r"The displayed left Maurer--Cartan form instead obeys \((\varphi\psi)^{-1}d(\varphi\psi)=\operatorname{Ad}\psi^{-1}(\varphi^{-1}d\varphi)+\psi^{-1}d\psi\). It cannot be paired with \(\operatorname{Ad}\varphi\) in the stated left-action convention.",
 r"The corrected cocycle appears in the energy construction \citep[Sec.~5]{wallach1987gauge}. Direct differentiation of a product independently verifies the correction; the original PNG confirms the manuscript order, so this is a faithful mathematical error.",["wallach1987gauge"],literal="false as written")
add(470,"P000864","construction","The energy representation",
 r"The notes form \(V_\varphi=U_{\alpha(\varphi)}\) from the Gaussian representation of the Euclidean group of \(\mathcal H\).",
 r"For a real orthogonal representation \(\pi\) and continuous one-cocycle \(\beta\), the bosonic Fock representation satisfies \(V_g e(h)=\exp(-\|\beta(g)\|^2/2-\langle\pi(g)h,\beta(g)\rangle)e(\pi(g)h+\beta(g))\), with exponential vectors normalized by \(\langle e(h),e(k)\rangle=\exp\langle h,k\rangle\). Apply this to the current-group action and corrected Maurer--Cartan cocycle.",
 r"The cocycle identity proves the representation law and the exponential-vector inner product proves unitarity. Inner-product conventions may conjugate the displayed scalar in a complex realization; the real Gaussian model fixes it unambiguously.",
 r"The energy representation was already constructed in \citet[Sec.~3]{albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy}; its later Fock formulation is checked in \citet[Sec.~5]{albeverioetal2015brownian}.",["albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy","albeverioetal2015brownian"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(470,"P000864","theorem","Irreducibility in dimension at least three",
 r"Theorem~1: \(V\) is irreducible for \(\dim X\ge3\), attributed to Albeverio and Høegh-Krohn after earlier bounds five and four.",
 r"For connected, orientable, paracompact smooth \(X\) of dimension at least three, compact semisimple \(G\), and the current-group energy representation from a Riemannian metric, smooth positive density and invariant positive Lie-algebra inner product, \(V\) is irreducible.",
 r"The lecture omits topological and measure hypotheses. Compactly supported currents and the full current group, rather than an arbitrary proper subgroup, are required.",
 r"The soon-after joint paper \citet{albeveriohoeghkrohntestard1981irreducibility} includes Testard. Its result and earlier restrictions are documented and extended to this generality by the fully read \citet[Theorem~6.2]{wallach1987gauge}. The notebook is an October 1979 occurrence; a November 1979 conference presentation is not treated as the first circulation.",["albeveriohoeghkrohntestard1981irreducibility","wallach1987gauge"],cls="A",match="exact")
add(470,"P000864-P000865","theorem","Reducibility in one dimension",
 r"Theorem~2 states reducibility for \(\dim X=1\), followed by the Brownian path model.",
 r"For the one-dimensional based path-group energy representation on a finite interval, the Brownian realization has non-scalar commuting right-action operators as well as the left-action representation. Hence the left representation is reducible for nontrivial \(G\).",
 r"Boundary and base-point conventions matter. This is the path model actually developed next in the notes, not an unqualified assertion for every subgroup or completion of a one-dimensional mapping group.",
 r"The 1981 Albeverio--Høegh-Krohn--Testard paper records reducibility. A directly checked later proof uses commuting left/right Brownian representations and their energy equivalence \citep[Theorems~4.6 and~5.8]{albeverioetal2015brownian}.",["albeveriohoeghkrohntestard1981irreducibility","albeverioetal2015brownian"],cls="A",match="partial")
energyid=add(470,"P000864","problem","Irreducibility on a two-dimensional base",
 r"\(\dim X=2\) is underlined and followed by a question mark.",
 r"For a connected smooth Riemannian surface \(X\), compact semisimple \(G\), positive smooth measure density and positive invariant inner product on \(\mathfrak g\), decide irreducibility of the ordinary current-group energy representation for arbitrary such data.",
 r"The parameters are part of the question: a result after rescaling the covariance or coupling is not an all-parameter solution. Projective positive-energy representations of a semidirect product with time evolution are a different representation class.",
 r"\citet[Theorem~6.2]{wallach1987gauge} proves irreducibility when \(d\mu=\rho\,d\operatorname{vol}\) and every root obeys \(\|\alpha\|_B>\sqrt{8\pi|\rho(x)|}\) at every \(x\). \citet{shimada2005irreducibility} claimed all dimensions, but \citet[p.~1]{hasebe2010white} identifies the invalid use of \(d\Phi=\beta(\exp\Phi)\) for nonabelian \(G\); \citet[Introduction]{ando2010local} also warns about the claim.",["wallach1987gauge","shimada2005irreducibility","hasebe2010white","ando2010local"],cls="C",status=r"As of November 2026, the verified sources establish a genuine parameter-restricted theorem, while the unrestricted two-dimensional claim remains unresolved within the checked literature. Targeted recent searches found no verified repair; this is not an exhaustive assertion of current global openness.",literal="not determinable",match="partial",problem="GP-1979-06",worth=r"The remaining distinction is mathematically sharp: remove the root/covariance bound without importing a flawed nonabelian differential identity or changing the representation.",confidence="medium")
add(470,"P000864-P000865","construction","Stochastic development to Brownian paths on a group",
 r"\(\eta^{-1}d\eta=db\); the notes say first to solve an ordinary equation and then transfer the measure. The displayed source space is accidentally \(C(\mathbb R^+,G)\).",
 r"Let \(b\) be Brownian motion in \(\mathfrak g\) for a positive invariant metric, with \(b_0=0\). On a finite interval, the Stratonovich equation \(\eta^{-1}\circ d\eta=db\), \(\eta_0=e\), defines group Brownian motion and a measurable stochastic-development map from Wiener paths in \(\mathfrak g\) to continuous paths in \(G\).",
 r"Brownian paths are not differentiable; the equation needs a stochastic convention. The input is Lie-algebra path space, and the measure \(\mu_0\) belongs there. The group output measure is its pushforward.",
 r"The construction is used in \citet[Sec.~4]{albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy}; the modern formulation and stochastic logarithms are explicit in \citet[Sec.~2]{albeverioetal2015brownian}.",["albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy","albeverioetal2015brownian"],literal="false as written")
add(470,"P000865","theorem","Quasi-invariance and the Brownian representation",
 r"Left multiplication \(\eta\mapsto\varphi\eta\) is assigned a Radon--Nikodym derivative for \(\varphi\in C^\infty(\mathbb R^+,G)\), producing \(W_\varphi\).",
 r"On based group Wiener space over \([0,T]\), multiplication by an absolutely continuous based path \(h\) with \(\int_0^T\|h^{-1}\dot h\|^2dt<\infty\) is quasi-invariant. Pullback multiplied by the square root of the appropriate Radon--Nikodym derivative is unitary.",
 r"The finite-energy condition is essential on an infinite interval. Smoothness alone on \(\mathbb R^+\) does not ensure finite energy. The initial value must preserve the based path space.",
 r"The precise finite-energy path group and its density are given in \citet[Sec.~2]{albeverioetal2015brownian}; this is the qualified version of the construction already used in 1978.",["albeverioetal2015brownian","albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(470,"P000865","theorem","Equivalence of Brownian and energy representations",
 r"Theorem: \(W_\varphi\cong V_\varphi\); the corresponding cocycle is the Maurer--Cartan form.",
 r"For a compact connected Lie group and the based finite-energy path group on a finite interval, the Brownian representation is unitarily equivalent to the energy representation with the same invariant metric. Left and right Brownian versions are related by inversion.",
 r"The comparison is between matching path groups, interval conventions and covariance normalizations. Stochastic logarithm followed by Fourier--Wiener transform provides an explicit intertwiner.",
 r"The equivalence is already central to \citet[Sec.~4]{albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy}. The full later construction is \citet[Theorem~5.8]{albeverioetal2015brownian}; its 2015 preprint is corroboration, not the first announcement.",["albeveriohoeghkrohn1978energy","albeverioetal2015brownian"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(470,"P000865","claim","The asserted type-III factor",
 r"\([L_\infty\cup W_\varphi]''\) is called a factor of type~3.",
 r"On \(L^2(Y,\mu)\) for a standard probability space, let \(L^\infty(Y,\mu)\) act by multiplication and let \(W\) be the quasi-regular unitaries of a nonsingular action. The commutant of \(\mathcal M=(L^\infty\cup W)''\) consists of invariant multiplication operators. If \(\mathcal M\) is a factor, that commutant is scalar, and \(\mathcal M=B(L^2(Y,\mu))\), a type-I factor.",
 r"Indeed the multiplication algebra is maximal abelian, so \(\mathcal M'\subset L^\infty\subset\mathcal M\). Factoriality forces \(\mathcal M'=\mathbb C I\). A group-measure-space crossed product acts on a different Hilbert space and must not be substituted silently.",
 r"The literal assertion is disproved by this elementary commutant argument. Later type-III factorial path-group representations \citep{albeverioetal1983factorial} concern a different generated algebra; that work is an indirect thematic lead, not an exact repair.",["albeverioetal1983factorial"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the printed operator-algebra expression is false under its stated realization. Which different factor construction was intended is not recoverable from the notebook.",literal="false as written",match="thematic only",verification="indirect",confidence="high")
prob("GP-1979-06","The two-dimensional energy representation",r"Is the ordinary energy representation of \(C^\infty_c(X,G)\) irreducible for every connected smooth surface \(X\), compact semisimple \(G\), and invariant positive covariance data?",[energyid],r"As of November 2026, a root/covariance inequality gives irreducibility (Wallach 1987, Theorem 6.2). The claimed all-dimensional proof by Shimada has an explicitly documented gap. No unrestricted solution was verified in targeted searches; this is a bounded literature-status conclusion, not an assertion of exhaustive current openness.",["wallach1987gauge","shimada2005irreducibility","hasebe2010white","ando2010local"])
