# Cambridge report: retain the distinction between identified work and oral topics.
add(462,"P000841","construction","Spacetime foam as a quantum-gravity proposal",
 r"Small-scale spacetime topology should include many little handles; Hawking's paper on spacetime foam is named.",
 r"The identifiable proposal is to include nontrivial four-dimensional topology among Euclidean gravitational configurations. The notes specify neither a measure on metrics modulo diffeomorphisms nor a convergent sum over topologies, and therefore state no rigorous existence theorem for a quantum theory.",
 r"This is a physical construction programme. A theorem claiming convergence of the gravitational functional integral would be an unjustified addition.",
 r"Hawking's named paper is already published in Nuclear Physics B 144 (1978), pp.~349--362 \citep{hawking1978foam}. The author's bibliography verifies the title, journal and date; its full argument was not retrieved.",["hawking1978foam"],literal="not determinable",match="exact",verification="publisher metadata checked",status=r"As of November 2026, the notebook is identifiable as exposition of that preexisting proposal, not as a precise unresolved mathematical problem.")
add(462,"P000841","claim","The Page--Atiyah graviton report",
 r"Page and Atiyah are associated with something involving \ru{изв.} or \ru{изл.} gravitons; the abbreviation is unclear.",
 r"The report does not identify an equation, geometric class or assertion. In particular, the adjective cannot securely be expanded to a unique type of gravitational instanton.",
 r"The original PNG and both transcriptions retain the ambiguity. No theorem has been manufactured from the two names.",
 r"Targeted searches for the Cambridge meeting and Page--Atiyah gravitational instantons did not verify an exact result matching this fragment.",[],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the specific oral result is unidentifiable from the retained text.",literal="not determinable",match="none found",verification="unresolved lead",confidence="low")
add(462,"P000841","claim","Coleman and Jackiw on topology and instanton methods",
 r"Coleman and Jackiw are said to discuss gravity with topological numbers and Yang--Mills; a survey of instanton methods is mentioned.",
 r"No particular topological invariant, gauge group, action or conclusion is recorded. The passage supplies conference provenance for instanton methods but not a uniquely reconstructable mathematical proposition.",
 r"Instanton-number quantization and tunnelling are different claims; neither is silently substituted for the fragment.",
 r"The topic is consistent with the period's instanton literature, but the cited survey and exact oral result were not identified. The appropriate classification is indeterminate, not an open problem.",[],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, exact publication identification remains unresolved in this search.",literal="not determinable",match="none found",verification="unresolved lead",confidence="low")
add(462,"P000841","claim","The four-manifold and bundle-classification report",
 r"Milnor: classification of four-dimensional manifolds; somebody else: bundles on four-manifolds.",
 r"The category (smooth, topological or homotopy), compactness, simple connectivity, orientation and type of bundle are all absent. No definite classification theorem follows from this topic line.",
 r"Later theorems with restricted hypotheses cannot be counted as resolving an unspecified request to classify all four-manifolds. The unnamed bundle contribution is retained in the same conference-topic item.",
 r"No exact theorem or publication was verified for this report. It remains provenance, not a claimed precursor to a particular later classification theorem.",[],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the recorded mathematical scope is insufficient for a unique fate.",literal="not determinable",match="none found",verification="unresolved lead",confidence="low")
add(462,"P000841","claim","Signature and characteristic numbers in dimension four",
 r"Singer is reported to have spoken on the signature of a four-manifold; Euler and Pontryagin numbers then occur in the index formula.",
 r"For a closed oriented smooth four-manifold \(M\), the signature of its middle-dimensional intersection pairing is \(\sigma(M)=\frac13\langle p_1(TM),[M]\rangle\). This is the standard signature theorem relevant to the report, not a reconstruction of any unstated new claim by Singer.",
 r"Closedness and orientation are necessary. Manifolds with boundary require additional boundary data and correction terms; the notebook supplies none.",
 r"The signature theorem is preexisting background, treated in \citet[Ch.~3]{gilkey1984invariance}. The exact Cambridge lecture may have contained more; its novelty is not recoverable here.",["gilkey1984invariance"],literal="not determinable",match="partial")
add(462,"P000841-P000842","claim","The recorded higher-spin zero-mode index formula",
 r"With \(S=A+B,T=A-B\), formula~(2) reads \(n[S;T]-n[s;T-1]=4^{s-1}[(T-\frac12)\chi+(s-\frac13)p/2]\), where \(n\) counts zero modes.",
 r"The meaningful index setting is an elliptic operator or elliptic complex on a closed oriented Riemannian four-manifold, with the required spin structure and specified higher-spin bundles. Its analytic index is an integral of characteristic classes. The exact displayed coefficient cannot be certified without fixing the operators and whether brackets denote unsymmetrized tensor powers.",
 r"P000853, formula~(39), instead has \(s-\frac23\). Both scans were inspected; the two expressions must not be silently identified. Bare zero-mode differences for unspecified Laplacians need not be topological indices.",
 r"The exact nearby publication is \citet{christensenduff1979indices}, July 1979, whose publisher abstract explicitly covers arbitrary-spin indices and heat coefficients. The full paper was inaccessible, so numerical agreement is unverified. Duff's later primary exposition confirms the programme \citep[Sec.~4]{duff1982ultraviolet}.",["christensenduff1979indices","duff1982ultraviolet"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the underlying higher-spin index theory is published, but this notebook formula's normalization and discrepancy remain unresolved.",literal="not determinable",match="partial",verification="indirect",confidence="medium")
add(462,"P000842","claim","Boson--fermion index identities on self-dual backgrounds",
 r"Formula~(3) relates \(n[A,B]\) and \(2n[A-\frac12,B]\) to \(4^{A+B-1}(A-\frac7{12})(\chi+p/2)\); the minus sign is overwritten. Self-duality is explicitly required.",
 r"On appropriately chosen self-dual Riemannian spin backgrounds, differential intertwiners can pair nonzero bosonic and fermionic spectra; their unmatched kernels produce an index relation. The concrete bundles, gauge constraints and topological normalizations must be supplied before a numerical formula is stated.",
 r"The overwritten sign and absent operator definitions prevent certifying the exact identity. The self-dual hypothesis is present in the notebook and is retained, rather than generalized to arbitrary metrics.",
 r"\citet{christensenduff1979indices} explicitly announces such supertheorems in its primary abstract. \citet[Secs.~2.3 and~4]{duff1982ultraviolet} supplies independently checked context; this is a partial match, not verification of the written coefficient.",["christensenduff1979indices","duff1982ultraviolet"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, a published family of results is identified, but this exact formula remains underdetermined.",literal="not determinable",match="partial",verification="indirect",confidence="medium")
add(462,"P000842","claim","Nonzero-mode cancellation and the heat coefficient",
 r"\(m[S;T]=m[S]=4^s m[0]\), \(B_4=n+m\); nonzero modes cancel in supergravity, leaving zero modes.",
 r"For a nonnegative elliptic Laplace-type operator \(P\) on a closed four-manifold, \(\zeta_P(s)=\sum_{\lambda>0}\lambda^{-s}\) has a continuation regular at zero, and the constant heat coefficient satisfies \(a_4(P)=\dim\ker P+\zeta_P(0)\). A verified spectral pairing can cancel the corresponding nonzero contributions in an appropriately weighted supertrace.",
 r"The term \(m\) must be a regularized spectral contribution, not a finite number of positive eigenvalues or a common multiplicity. P000852 explicitly rejects the latter interpretation. The power \(4^s\) needs the tensor-space convention before it can be checked.",
 r"The general heat/zeta identity is established \citep[Secs.~1.7 and~1.10]{gilkey1984invariance}; the self-dual supergravity application is discussed in \citet{duff1982ultraviolet}. The numerical spin factor remains only a partial publication match.",["gilkey1984invariance","duff1982ultraviolet"],match="partial")
add(462,"P000842","claim","Finiteness in a self-dual supergravity sector",
 r"They proved that the functional integral has no divergences; the speaker obtained related results.",
 r"The identified perturbative claim concerns local ultraviolet counterterms evaluated on backgrounds obeying specified self-duality conditions. It is not a construction of a nonperturbative gravitational measure or a claim that every supergravity amplitude on every background is finite.",
 r"The preceding sentence restricts the calculation to a self-dual sector. Gauge fixing, zero modes, regularization, loop order and the precise supergravity theory are absent from the shorthand.",
 r"\citet{christensenduff1979indices} states a beyond-one-loop self-dual result; \citet{ k allosh1980selfduality} concerns superspace self-duality. The latter citation key is normalized in authoring. Duff's checked 1982 lectures, Sec.~2.3, explain both the self-dual cancellation and allowed counterterms outside that restriction \citep{duff1982ultraviolet}.",["christensenduff1979indices","kallosh1980selfduality","duff1982ultraviolet"],cls="A",literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial",verification="indirect",status=r"As of November 2026, the qualified sector-specific publication is identified; the unrestricted reading is unsupported.")
add(462,"P000842","problem","An unspecified super-index theorem",
 r"Interesting problem: the same on a supermanifold; Singer is working on it; make a super index theorem.",
 r"No super-elliptic operator, symbol class, function space, compactness condition or index target is given. Since odd-coordinate differential calculi differ from ordinary elliptic theory, these choices determine the theorem.",
 r"This is a research programme, not a single well-posed conjecture. It is kept distinct from the concrete self-dual boson--fermion identities.",
 r"The later superanalysis framework \citep{berezin1987superanalysis} supplies language, but no exact result can be matched to the unspecified request.",["berezin1987superanalysis"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the notebook does not determine a unique problem whose resolution can be recorded.",literal="not determinable",match="thematic only",verification="publisher metadata checked",confidence="high")
add(462,"P000842","claim","Exceptional symmetry of maximal supergravity",
 r"The French group replaces an unsatisfactory \(SO(8)\) by \(E_7\longrightarrow SU(8)\); \(\dim E_7=133\), with an abbreviation possibly meaning compact.",
 r"In ungauged four-dimensional maximal supergravity the relevant continuous exceptional symmetry is the split real group \(E_{7(7)}\), with maximal compact subgroup locally \(SU(8)\); the scalar manifold has local form \(E_{7(7)}/SU(8)\), of dimension \(133-63=70\).",
 r"The arrow is not a specified homomorphism or symmetry-breaking theorem. The compact real form of \(E_7\) would be the wrong real form for this scalar coset. Gauged \(SO(8)\) supergravity is a related but distinct construction.",
 r"The preexisting Cremmer--Julia work of 1978 is identified in \citet[supergravity field-content and symmetry discussion]{duff1982ultraviolet}. The independently opened historical source verifies the context, but no exact meaning is assigned to the notebook arrow.",["duff1982ultraviolet"],match="partial",literal="correct with missing hypotheses",verification="indirect")
add(462,"P000842","theorem","The exceptional Jordan algebra and compact F4",
 r"Take Hermitian \(3\times3\) octonionic matrices with product \((AB+BA)/2\); their automorphism group is \(F_4\).",
 r"For the real division octonions \(\mathbb O\), the Albert algebra \(J=\operatorname{Herm}_3(\mathbb O)\), with \(A\circ B=(AB+BA)/2\), is a 27-dimensional real Jordan algebra. Its real automorphism group is the compact real form of \(F_4\).",
 r"Only products of two octonions occur in the displayed matrix product, so it is well defined despite nonassociativity. Split octonions or complexification change the real form.",
 r"This is a classical result, not new in 1979. \citet[Sec.~4.2, Theorem~5]{baez2002octonions} gives the precise statement and credits the 1950 Chevalley--Schafer construction.",["baez2002octonions"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")

# Zakharov and Arnold: distinguish geometry, formal tests and actual integrability.
add(463,"P000843","definition","Additional additive functions on a resonance manifold",
 r"\(\Gamma=\{(k_1,k_2):\omega(k_1+k_2)=\omega(k_1)+\omega(k_2)\}\); seek another \(f\) additive there and not differing from \(\omega\) by a linear function.",
 r"For a smooth real dispersion \(\omega\) on an open momentum domain, restrict to a nonempty regular component of the three-wave resonance equation. An additional collision invariant is a smooth \(f\) satisfying the same additivity relation and lying outside \(\{a\omega+b\cdot k:a\in\mathbb R,b\in\mathbb R^N\}\).",
 r"Nonlinearity alone does not guarantee a smooth hypersurface or even a nonempty resonance set. The quotient by all scalar multiples of \(\omega\) is essential: otherwise \(2\omega\) is spuriously additional.",
 r"The definition is made precise in the speaker's subsequent work \citep[Sec.~3, equations~(3.8)--(3.9)]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}, following the 1980 paper \citep{zakharovschulman1980degenerative}.",["zakharovschulman1980degenerative","zakharovschulman1988invariants"],cls="A",literal="false as written")
add(463,"P000843-P000844","construction","Difference curves yield families of collision invariants",
 r"\(p=\xi_1-\xi_2,\ q=a(\xi_1)-a(\xi_2),\ \omega=b(\xi_1)-b(\xi_2)\); replace \(b\) by arbitrary \(c\) to obtain \(f\). Three parameters telescope along \((\xi_1,\xi_2,\xi_3)\).",
 r"If \(a,b,c\) are smooth and \(a'(\xi_1)\ne a'(\xi_2)\), the first two expressions define local momentum coordinates. The associated functions \(\omega,f\) obey the three-wave identity on composable pairs \((\xi_1,\xi_2),(\xi_2,\xi_3)\), wherever the chosen local branches are compatible.",
 r"The determinant of the coordinate map is \(a'(\xi_1)-a'(\xi_2)\). Telescoping proves the identity on the constructed component; it does not prove additivity on every disconnected resonance component or global single-valuedness.",
 r"The construction appears in \citet[Sec.~3, equations~(3.10)--(3.11)]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}. That paper explicitly distinguishes partial from complete degeneracy.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants"],cls="A",literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
resonanceid=add(463,"P000843","problem","Is the difference-curve construction universal?",
 r"The page first asks for one additional additive function, then frames the construction as obtaining equations with infinitely many first integrals, and finally asks whether it is general.",
 r"In the infinitely-many-invariants reading, ask whether a regular local two-dimensional three-wave dispersion law admitting infinitely many independent smooth collision invariants can be represented by \((p,q,\omega)=(\xi_1-\xi_2,a(\xi_1)-a(\xi_2),b(\xi_1)-b(\xi_2))\), with an invertible momentum parametrization and compatible resonance branches.",
 r"The initial one-extra-invariant question and the subsequent infinitely-many-invariants programme are different quantifiers. The former cannot silently replace the latter. Additivity on a selected local component must also be distinguished from additivity on the entire resonance set and from nonlinear conserved quantities of the full wave equation.",
 r"The speaker's construction is published in \citet[Sec.~3]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}. Later \citet[abstract]{balkferapontov1994invariants} explicitly classifies three-wave interactions with infinitely many independent invariants using web geometry; the original theorem and its hypotheses were not retrieved, so this remains a partial publication match. The earlier none-or-infinitely-many expectation is contradicted by the finite-invariant examples announced in \citet[Secs.~4--6]{balknazarenkozakharov1991drift}. Their uniqueness proof is deferred there; \citet[Sec.~2.1]{balk2014rossby} confirms the Rossby uniqueness result and cites \citet{balkferapontov1998web}.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants","balkferapontov1994invariants","balknazarenkozakharov1991drift","balk2014rossby","balkferapontov1998web"],cls="B",status=r"As of November 2026, the infinitely-many-invariants classification has an identified later publication. An exact hypothesis-by-hypothesis comparison with the handwritten parametrization remains unverified because the 1994 theorem was inaccessible. The broader one-invariant reading is distinct; finite-extra-invariant systems occur in the published literature. No current open problem is inferred from the access limit.",literal="not determinable",match="partial",verification="indirect",problem="GP-1979-05",confidence="medium")
add(463,"P000844","construction","Zero curvature and the dispersion of a dressed scalar pair",
 r"\(L_t-A_y+[L,A]=0\), with scalar differential operators in \(x\); linearization at \(L_0,A_0\) has constant coefficients and difference-symbol dispersion.",
 r"Take constant-coefficient scalar operators \(L_0=\ell(\partial_x)\), \(A_0=a(\partial_x)\), and linearize a compatible pair \(\partial_y-L,\partial_t-A\). Plane-wave or exponential perturbations give algebraic relations expressible using differences of the two symbols in the standard dressing construction.",
 r"The zero-curvature sign depends on the chosen auxiliary operators. Linearization has constant coefficients only about a constant background; variable background coefficients do not disappear merely by discarding nonlinear terms.",
 r"The preexisting dressing framework is \citet{zakharovshabat1974scheme}. Its exact bibliographic metadata was checked; the notebook's particular symbols and linearization were verified algebraically rather than attributed to a separately read theorem.",["zakharovshabat1974scheme"],verification="publisher metadata checked")
add(463,"P000844","construction","The linearized Gel'fand--Levitan--Marchenko equation",
 r"\(F+K+K\oplus F=0\), followed by \(K=-F\) and equations for the \(t,y\) dependence of \(F\).",
 r"Interpret \(K\oplus F\) as the integral-operator composition appropriate to a chosen triangular dressing factorization. For a small kernel \(F\), \(K=-F+O(F^2)\). The equality \(K=-F\) is exact only at first order or when the composition term vanishes.",
 r"The displayed differential equations attach \(t\) to \(L_0\) and \(y\) to \(A_0\), while the exponential attaches \(y\) to \(\ell\) and \(t\) to \(a\); their signs also depend on action on the second kernel variable. The transcript is retained and the inconsistent assignments are not silently reconciled.",
 r"The GLM/dressing method predates this lecture \citep{zakharovshabat1974scheme}; the first-order identity follows directly by retaining degree one in the kernel equation.",["zakharovshabat1974scheme"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial",verification="publisher metadata checked")
add(463,"P000845-P000846","theorem","The resonance obstruction for an additional quadratic-leading integral",
 r"If the equation is integrable and its dispersion nondegenerate, the interaction vanishes on the resonance manifold; the notes also question integrals beginning at higher orders.",
 r"For an analytic translation-invariant Hamiltonian wave system admitting an additional analytic integral whose leading term is \(\int f(k)|a_k|^2dk\), each on-shell scattering amplitude \(W\) satisfies \((\sum_{\rm in}f-\sum_{\rm out}f)W=0\) on its regular resonance set. Thus either the additional additive identity holds there or that amplitude vanishes.",
 r"The hypotheses concern a specified analytic expansion with a quadratic leading integral and regular coefficients. They do not identify all notions of integrability, and do not automatically cover integrals whose first nonzero term has higher degree.",
 r"This is the substantive later theorem of \citet[Sec.~2 and Theorem~3.1]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}, announced in the 1980 work. The checked paper carefully distinguishes formal scattering series, analytic invariants and completeness.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants","zakharovschulman1980degenerative"],cls="A",literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(463,"P000844-P000845","construction","The KP dispersion from quadratic and cubic symbols",
 r"\(\ell(\xi)=\xi^2,\ a(\xi)=\xi^3\) is first called KdV and then KP; \(\omega=\frac14(p^3+3q^2/p)\).",
 r"With \(p=\xi_1-\xi_2\), \(q=\xi_1^2-\xi_2^2\) and \(p\ne0\), one has \(\xi_1=(q/p+p)/2,\ \xi_2=(q/p-p)/2\), hence \(\xi_1^3-\xi_2^3=(p^3+3q^2/p)/4\). This is the two-spatial-variable KP symbol in the lecture's exponential convention; a \(y\)-independent reduction yields KdV after scaling.",
 r"The rational expression is undefined at \(p=0\). The written KP evolution lacks its final equality to zero, and switching from real exponentials to oscillatory Fourier modes changes displayed signs.",
 r"Scalar dressing by these symbols is part of the established Zakharov--Shabat framework \citep{zakharovshabat1974scheme}; the exact algebraic identity is verified directly. The resonance geometry is discussed in \citet[Sec.~3]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}.",["zakharovshabat1974scheme","zakharovschulman1988invariants"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(463,"P000845","construction","A matrix dressing pair and the Davey--Stewartson system",
 r"Dressing \(\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\partial_x\) and \(I\partial_x^2\) yields a two-component operator with off-diagonal \(\psi,\bar\psi\), then the displayed hyperbolic Schrödinger/elliptic potential system.",
 r"The recorded system is \(i\psi_t-\psi_{xx}+\psi_{yy}+u\psi=0,\quad \Delta u=(\partial_x^2-\partial_y^2)|\psi|^2\), with real \(u\). In a decaying setting the second equation determines \(u\) up to an imposed harmonic component. It belongs, after coefficient and sign conventions are fixed, to the Davey--Stewartson family generated by matrix dressing.",
 r"The exact form of the dressed \(A\), reduction signs, boundary conditions and nonlinear coefficient normalization are omitted. Consequently the displayed pair is not by itself a verified complete Lax representation.",
 r"The family is explicitly discussed in the primary later account \citep[Secs.~1 and~4]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}; the earlier dressing source is \citet{zakharovshabat1974scheme}. This is a partial match to the particular shorthand system.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants","zakharovshabat1974scheme"],match="partial")
add(463,"P000845","construction","Fourier Hamiltonians and the complex variational derivative",
 r"The Fourier equation is written \(ia_t=\delta H/\delta a\); the quartic interaction integral displays only two integration differentials.",
 r"For complex canonical wave variables with the usual Poisson convention, \(i\partial_t a(k)=\delta H/\delta\overline{a(k)}\). A quartic translation-invariant Hamiltonian integrates \(\bar a_1\bar a_2a_3a_4\) against a kernel and \(\delta(k_1+k_2-k_3-k_4)\), with all four momenta integrated, or three after eliminating the delta constraint.",
 r"The missing conjugation is a mathematical error in the displayed canonical equation. Omitting integration variables is shorthand, but must not be interpreted as a two-variable integral with free momenta.",
 r"The precise convention is explicit in \citet[Sec.~1, equation~(1.1)]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}; it also follows from varying the quadratic term \(H_0=\int\omega|a|^2\).",["zakharovschulman1988invariants"],literal="false as written")
add(463,"P000845-P000846","claim","The displayed four-wave coefficient does not vanish on resonance",
 r"The two rational fractions printed for \(w\) are followed by \(w|_\Gamma=0\), with energy and momentum conservation defining \(\Gamma\).",
 r"For the printed expression and \(\omega(p,q)=-p^2+q^2\), take \(k_1=(1,0),k_2=(0,1),k_3=(0,0),k_4=(1,1)\). Both conservation laws hold and all denominators are nonzero, but the two fractions give \(1+1/5=6/5\), so the written vanishing claim is false.",
 r"The original PNG confirms plus signs inside the second denominator. Replacing those signs by minus signs alone still fails: it gives \(2\) at this quartet. A correct symmetrized scattering amplitude requires additional index or term changes, which were not reconstructed from the notes.",
 r"The later scattering-amplitude theorem \citep{zakharovschulman1988invariants} provides the appropriate context but does not certify this erroneous displayed kernel. The explicit counterexample is checked in this chapter.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the printed formula is disproved; an exact source-level restoration of the intended coefficient remains unverified.",literal="false as written",match="thematic only",confidence="high")
add(463,"P000846","claim","Poincaré's first-order homological obstruction",
 r"For \(H=H_0(p)+\varepsilon\sum h_k(p)e^{ikq}\), a nondegenerate frequency map is assumed and the notes state \(h_k=0\) on \(k\cdot\lambda=0\); infinitely many violations supposedly exclude an independent integral.",
 r"If \(F=F_0(p)+\varepsilon F_1(p,q)+\cdots\) is an analytic or formal integral, the first-order homological equation gives \((k\cdot\nabla H_0)F_{1,k}=(k\cdot\nabla F_0)h_k\), up to a common sign convention. Therefore \((k\cdot\nabla F_0)h_k=0\) on each resonance.",
 r"The factor involving \(F_0\) cannot be dropped. A nonintegrability theorem additionally needs a sufficiently rich Poincaré set of independent resonances and a nondegenerate Hessian; merely counting infinitely many nonzero coefficients is insufficient.",
 r"The classical perturbative context is the one explicitly compared with the wave theorem by \citet[Introduction]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}. Arnold's textbook metadata and appendix context were checked \citep{arnold1989mechanics}; the displayed necessary condition is derived here, not quoted from an unread proof.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants","arnold1989mechanics"],literal="false as written",match="partial",speaker="V. I. Arnold")
add(463,"P000846","construction","Translation-surface interpretation of the resonance parametrization",
 r"Arnold interprets the difference graph as translating one curve along another; Zakharov writes a three-branch resonance law \(\omega_1(k_1+k_2)=\omega_2(k_1)+\omega_3(k_2)\).",
 r"The graph parametrized by \((p,q,\omega)=(\xi_1,a(\xi_1),b(\xi_1))-(\xi_2,a(\xi_2),b(\xi_2))\) is the local difference of two curve arcs, hence a translation surface where its differential has rank two. Distinct branches lead to analogous sums of possibly different curves.",
 r"This is a geometric interpretation and local construction. It does not classify every three-branch functional equation without transversality and compatibility hypotheses.",
 r"The single-branch construction is verified in \citet[Sec.~3]{zakharovschulman1988invariants}; the general translation-geometry language is classical \citep[Sec.~2]{little1983translation}.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants","little1983translation"],match="partial",speaker="V. I. Arnold and V. E. Zakharov")
add(463,"P000846","problem","Integrals with higher-order leading terms",
 r"What if the integrals start directly at higher orders?",
 r"The question asks whether a resonance test can detect conserved quantities with no quadratic leading term, but gives no order, regularity class, Hamiltonian class or desired criterion. The quadratic-leading theorem does not answer it.",
 r"Once those choices are fixed, expanding \(\{H,F\}=0\) gives degree-specific homological equations; different choices produce different problems.",
 r"\citet[Sec.~1, equation~(1.3)]{zakharovschulman1988invariants} explicitly starts with a quadratic leading invariant. Its theorem is therefore not a solution of this broader, unspecified request.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, this fragment is a legitimate question about the theorem's scope but is insufficiently specified for a unique global publication fate.",literal="not determinable",match="partial",confidence="medium")
add(463,"P000847","theorem","Formal Birkhoff normal form at a nonresonant elliptic equilibrium",
 r"Arnold: \(H=\sum\omega_k z_k\bar z_k+\cdots\) reduces to \(H(\Pi_k)\), \(\Pi_k=z_k\bar z_k\), if the frequencies are independent; explicitly at the level of formal series.",
 r"For a formal Hamiltonian near an elliptic equilibrium with rationally independent frequencies, a formal symplectic change of variables puts it into a power series in the actions \(I_k=z_k\bar z_k\). Rational independence means \(m\cdot\omega\ne0\) for every nonzero \(m\in\mathbb Z^n\).",
 r"At each degree the homological operator is diagonal on monomials; nonresonant terms can be divided by their nonzero frequency combinations. The remaining monomials have equal powers of \(z_k,\bar z_k\). No convergence or analytic integrability follows.",
 r"This is the classical Birkhoff theorem summarized in Arnold's mechanics text, Appendix~7 \citep{arnold1989mechanics}. Publisher metadata was checked; the finite-degree elimination explanation above independently verifies the formal assertion.",["arnold1989mechanics"],literal="correct",verification="publisher metadata checked",speaker="V. I. Arnold")
prob("GP-1979-05","Universality of the difference-curve resonance construction",r"Does every regular local two-dimensional three-wave dispersion law with infinitely many independent collision invariants admit the difference-curve parametrization on compatible resonance branches? This reconstructs the immediately preceding infinitely-many-first-integrals programme; it is not the earlier, broader request for one additional invariant.",[resonanceid],r"As of November 2026, Balk--Ferapontov's 1994 publication explicitly gives a classification of three-wave interactions with infinitely many invariants. Its full theorem was not retrieved, so exact local and branch hypotheses are unverified. The one-extra-invariant reading is distinct: finite-invariant examples were announced in 1991 and their uniqueness is discussed in the 1998 web-geometry development and Balk2014, Sec.2.1. This is a developed historical question with partial publication matching, not a claim of current openness.",["zakharovschulman1988invariants","balkferapontov1994invariants","balknazarenkozakharov1991drift","balkferapontov1998web","balk2014rossby"])
