add(465,"P000851","construction","Differential forms and the de Rham Laplacian",
 r"\(w=(1/p!)\omega_{\mu_1\ldots\mu_p}dx^{\mu_1}\wedge\cdots\wedge dx^{\mu_p}\), \(\Delta=d\delta+\delta d\), with scalar, vector and two-form spin types listed.",
 r"On a closed oriented Riemannian manifold, \(d\) and its \(L^2\)-adjoint \(\delta\) define a nonnegative elliptic Laplacian on forms; its kernel in degree \(p\) represents \(H^p_{\mathrm{dR}}\). In dimension four the two-form representation splits into \((1,0)\) and \((0,1)\).",
 r"The notebook's language that forms describe bosons concerns transformation type. Form degree, Grassmann parity of field coefficients and physical statistics are different gradings.",
 r"This is preexisting Hodge theory \citep[Sec.~1.5]{gilkey1984invariance}; the displayed coordinate expression is correct.",["gilkey1984invariance"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(465,"P000851","problem","Mixed-parity differential forms and an index theorem",
 r"Needed: anticommuting \(dx\), then forms with commuting and anticommuting differentials, an operator \(\Delta\), and an index theorem; Gelfand says it need not be \(d\delta+\delta d\).",
 r"On a supermanifold, the parity-shifted tangent construction has odd \(dx\) for even coordinates \(x\) and even \(d\theta\) for odd coordinates \(\theta\). This gives a differential calculus, but no particular Fredholm complex or index theorem is determined by the notebook.",
 r"Ordinary exterior differentials already anticommute. The substantive requested extension is the mixed parity calculus and a suitable analytical index, with domains, compactness and symbol assumptions still absent.",
 r"The Berezin--Leites supermanifold framework predates the lecture \citep{berezinleites1975supermanifolds}; its existence does not resolve the unspecified analytic request. This repeats the programme of 1979-462.10.",["berezinleites1975supermanifolds"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the construction language is established but no unique super-index problem can be inferred.",literal="not determinable",match="thematic only",verification="indirect",speaker="Renata Kallosh and I. M. Gelfand")
add(465,"P000851","claim","The spin-five-halves gravitational-coupling obstruction",
 r"For spin \(5/2\), none of the equations will do because an interaction cannot be constructed; for spin \(3/2\) it has just been done in supergravity.",
 r"Minimal coupling of an isolated massless spin-\(5/2\) field to a general gravitational background introduces Weyl-curvature terms in the gauge consistency conditions. This obstructs the naive coupling; it is not a theorem excluding every nonminimal, self-dual, lower-dimensional or infinite-tower theory.",
 r"The scope restriction matters even in the 1979 source: the identified paper explicitly discusses self-dual exceptions.",
 r"\citet{aragonedeser1979hypergravity}, published 24 September 1979, is the directly relevant preexisting result. Its primary publisher abstract was checked, but the full three-page proof was not retrieved.",["aragonedeser1979hypergravity"],literal="false as written",match="partial",verification="indirect")
add(465,"P000852","theorem","Reducible tensor powers versus irreducible spin labels",
 r"\([1,0]=(1,0)\oplus(0,0)\), with a two-index spinor split into symmetric and epsilon-trace parts.",
 r"For a two-dimensional spin module \(S\), \(S\otimes S=\operatorname{Sym}^2S\oplus\Lambda^2S\), with dimensions \(4=3+1\). Thus brackets can consistently denote an unsymmetrized tensor power, while parentheses denote an irreducible spin type.",
 r"The square brackets are essential; reading both sides as irreducible labels would create a false equation. The trace sign in the indexed formula depends on the epsilon raising convention.",
 r"This is elementary preexisting representation theory \citep[Sec.~3.2]{gilkey1984invariance}. It explains why the powers \(4^S\) in the later shorthand need not be dimensions of irreducible \((A,B)\) modules.",["gilkey1984invariance"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(465,"P000852-P000853","claim","Compatibility and spectral intertwiners",
 r"Symmetry and the eigenvalue equation imply a compatibility identity; \([\nabla,\Delta]\phi=0\), and differentiating changes \([A,B]\) to \([A-\frac12,B+\frac12]\), preserving nonzero modes.",
 r"An operator \(Q:E\to F\) intertwines two Laplacians if \(QP_E=P_FQ\). To obtain an isomorphism of positive-eigenvalue spaces one also needs an inverse relation such as \(Q^*Q=P_E\) and \(QQ^*=P_F\), or its appropriately normalized counterpart. Then \(Q/\sqrt\lambda\) is an isomorphism on each \(\lambda>0\) eigenspace.",
 r"Covariant derivatives do not commute with Laplacians on arbitrary curved backgrounds. The special curvature/compatibility assumptions are indispensable; an intertwiner alone may have a nontrivial kernel.",
 r"The identified higher-spin/self-dual work concerns such relations \citep{christensenduff1979indices,hawkingpope1978symmetry}; exact agreement with equations~(27), (33)--(36) was not verified. The rigorous conditional operator lemma above is checked directly.",["christensenduff1979indices","hawkingpope1978symmetry"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the mechanism is established but the notebook does not supply enough hypotheses to certify its particular differential intertwiner.",literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial",verification="indirect",confidence="medium")
add(465,"P000852-P000853","theorem","Heat-kernel expansion and its spectral realization",
 r"The heat equation has delta initial data, a four-dimensional expansion \(\sum b_k t^{(k-4)/2}\), and a sum over orthonormal eigenfunctions.",
 r"For a nonnegative self-adjoint Laplace-type operator \(P\) on a Hermitian bundle over a closed four-manifold, \(K_t(x,x')=\sum_j e^{-t\lambda_j}\phi_j(x)\otimes\phi_j(x')^*\). Its trace has asymptotic expansion \(\sum_{k\ge0}B_k t^{(k-4)/2}\), with \(B_k=\int\operatorname{tr}b_k(x)\,d\operatorname{vol}\); odd-index coefficients vanish in the closed case.",
 r"The adjoint on the second eigenfunction and fibre trace are required for complex vector bundles. The sum converges smoothly for \(t>0\), and the delta initial condition is distributional.",
 r"Standard spectral/heat theory, fully treated in \citet[Secs.~1.6--1.7]{gilkey1984invariance}. The malformed exponent in (43) is fixed by (29) and the dimension.",["gilkey1984invariance"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(465,"P000852-P000853","claim","The regularized nonzero-mode contribution need not be an integer",
 r"\(B_4=n+m\); the notebook first conjectures a common multiplicity, explicitly marks it incorrect, then calls \(m\) a regularized sum and nevertheless an integer.",
 r"The correct quantity is \(m=\zeta_P(0)=B_4-\dim\ker P\). It generally is not an integer. For the scalar Laplacian on the unit round \(S^4\), the standard local coefficient gives \(B_4=29/90\) and \(m=-61/90\), since the kernel consists of constants.",
 r"For that sphere \(R=12\), \(|\operatorname{Ric}|^2=36\), \(|\operatorname{Riem}|^2=24\), and \(\operatorname{vol}=8\pi^2/3\). Substituting in \((360(4\pi)^2)^{-1}\int(5R^2-2|\operatorname{Ric}|^2+2|\operatorname{Riem}|^2)\) yields \(29/90\). An index supertrace may be integral even when individual regularized counts are not.",
 r"The heat/zeta relation and coefficient formula are in \citet[Secs.~1.10 and~4.8]{gilkey1984invariance}. The notebook's own rejection of the multiplicity interpretation is retained, and the later integrality claim is independently disproved.",["gilkey1984invariance"],literal="false as written",match="partial")
add(465,"P000852","claim","Heat coefficients through order six and the boundary caveat",
 r"Gilkey has computed through \(B_6\), with 1975 and a correction mentioning 1979; boundary \(b_4\) has recently been written.",
 r"For Laplace-type operators on closed manifolds, local heat coefficients through order six are universal curvature/endomorphism expressions. With boundary, a coefficient is not determined until an elliptic boundary condition is specified; it includes boundary invariants as well as interior terms.",
 r"The exact 1979 reference is recoverable. The anonymous boundary claim is not: Dirichlet, Robin, mixed and spectral boundary conditions yield different statements.",
 r"\citet{gilkey1979recursion}, Compositio 38, pp.~201--240, is the verified 1979 paper; the later author monograph \citep[Sec.~4.8]{gilkey1984invariance} gives explicit formulae. The unspecified boundary contribution remains only a thematic match.",["gilkey1979recursion","gilkey1984invariance"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial")
add(465,"P000853","claim","Index differences after spectral cancellation",
 r"If \(m[S,T]\) is independent of \(T\), then \(n[S,T]-n[S,T']=B_4[S,T]-B_4[S,T']\), followed by formula~(39).",
 r"For two operators satisfying equality of their regularized nonzero-mode contributions, subtracting \(B_4=\dim\ker+\zeta(0)\) gives the displayed kernel-dimension difference. To turn it into a topological formula one needs a verified elliptic-complex or spectral-pairing construction and the correct heat coefficients.",
 r"This conditional subtraction is valid, but the numerical factor in (39) is not independently verified and disagrees with P000841: \(s-\frac23\) versus \(s-\frac13\). The distinction between tensor powers and irreducible bundles is unresolved.",
 r"The general mechanism is established heat-index theory \citep[Sec.~1.7]{gilkey1984invariance}; the exact proposed higher-spin family matches the topic of \citet{christensenduff1979indices}, not a checked coefficient-by-coefficient theorem.",["gilkey1984invariance","christensenduff1979indices"],cls="F",status=r"As of November 2026, the algebraic subtraction is verified, while the notebook's numerical higher-spin identity remains uncertain.",literal="not determinable",match="partial",verification="indirect",confidence="medium")
add(465,"P000853","claim","Euler and Pontryagin integrals",
 r"\(\chi=\int {}^*R_{abcd}\,{}^*R^{abcd}\,d\operatorname{vol}\) and \(p=\int{}^*R_{abcd}R^{abcd}\,d\operatorname{vol}\), without constants.",
 r"On a closed oriented Riemannian four-manifold, \(\chi(M)=(32\pi^2)^{-1}\int(|\operatorname{Riem}|^2-4|\operatorname{Ric}|^2+R^2)\,d\operatorname{vol}\). With curvature two-form matrix \(\Omega\), \(p_1(TM)=-(8\pi^2)^{-1}\operatorname{tr}(\Omega\wedge\Omega)\) in the stated Chern--Weil convention.",
 r"Applying the same Hodge star to both factors preserves their inner product and gives \(|R|^2\), which is not the general Euler density. Ricci-flatness simplifies the Euler formula but does not remove its normalization. The Pontryagin scalar contraction also needs normalization and orientation convention.",
 r"These are classical Chern--Weil and Gauss--Bonnet formulae \citep[Secs.~2.2, 2.4 and~3.1]{gilkey1984invariance}. The PNG confirms both stars in the first printed formula.",["gilkey1984invariance"],literal="false as written",match="partial")

add(466,"P000854","problem","The two Gaussian Fourier exercises",
 r"Approximate \(\int e^{-x^2}\cos1000x\,dx\) and \(\int e^{-1000x^2}\cos x\,dx\), over the full real line.",
 r"For \(a>0\), \(\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-ax^2}\cos(bx)\,dx=\sqrt{\pi/a}\,e^{-b^2/(4a)}\). Thus the answers are \(\sqrt\pi\,e^{-250000}\) and \(\sqrt{\pi/1000}\,e^{-1/4000}\), respectively.",
 r"The exact Fourier formula follows by differentiating in \(b\), integrating by parts to obtain \(I'=-bI/(2a)\), and using the Gaussian integral at \(b=0\). The first integral is positive but extraordinarily small; coarse oscillatory quadrature is unsuitable.",
 r"Elementary preexisting Fourier analysis; the transform normalization is consistent with \citet[Sec.~1.1]{gilkey1984invariance}. The exercise is solved explicitly here, with no research-problem status.",["gilkey1984invariance"],literal="correct")
add(466,"P000854","problem","The narrow quartic Fourier exercise",
 r"Approximate \(\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-1000x^4}\cos x\,dx\).",
 r"For \(a>0\), \(I(a,b)=\frac1{2a^{1/4}}\sum_{k\ge0}\frac{(-1)^k\Gamma((2k+1)/4)}{(2k)!}(ba^{-1/4})^{2k}\). At \(a=1000,b=1\), this gives approximately \(0.32064797070715458\).",
 r"Termwise integration follows from domination by \(e^{-ax^4+|b||x|}\). The series converges for every finite \(b\). The value was checked independently by direct high-precision quadrature; the small scaled frequency makes this stable.",
 r"An elementary scaling and Taylor-series exercise, fully resolved by the displayed derivation. No exact historical publication is required for the calculation.",[],literal="correct",verification="read the result")
add(466,"P000854","problem","The highly oscillatory quartic Fourier exercise",
 r"Approximate \(\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-x^4}\cos1000x\,dx\).",
 r"Put \(r=(b/4)^{1/3}\). As \(b\to+\infty\), \(I(1,b)=\sqrt{2\pi/3}\,r^{-1}e^{-3r^4/2}\{\cos(3\sqrt3\,r^4/2-\pi/6)+O(r^{-4})\}\). At \(b=1000\), shifted-contour quadrature gives approximately \(1.7020109384\times10^{-1027}\); the leading saddle expression gives \(1.7019868243\times10^{-1027}\).",
 r"The contributing saddles of \(-z^4+ibz\) are \(r e^{i\pi/6}\) and \(r e^{5i\pi/6}\). Shifting the real contour to height \(r/2\) makes their exponentially small contribution numerically accessible. The error is absolute inside the braces, not a uniform relative error near zeros of the cosine.",
 r"The exercise is resolved by elementary steepest descent and a separately saved numerical check. The convergent series in the preceding item is exact but suffers severe cancellation at this frequency.",[],literal="correct",verification="read the result")
add(466,"P000854","problem","Canonical form of a symplectically self-adjoint operator",
 r"Find the canonical form of a self-adjoint operator in a symplectic space; the adjective is abbreviated in the manuscript.",
 r"Over \(\mathbb C\), let \(\omega\) be a nondegenerate alternating form and \(A\) satisfy \(\omega(Au,v)=\omega(u,Av)\). There is a symplectic basis in which \(A=\operatorname{diag}(J,J^{\mathsf T})\), with \(J\) in Jordan normal form. Hence each Jordan block occurs with its paired transpose.",
 r"This is self-adjointness for the alternating form, not the skew-adjoint Hamiltonian condition. Over a perfect field of characteristic not two, an analogous polarization holds; a Jordan matrix over the base field additionally requires split characteristic polynomial.",
 r"The exact result is proved in \citet[Theorem~1 and Corollary~11]{sanabria2017symplectic}, which explicitly identifies earlier classification results. This is a standard linear-algebra exercise rather than a claim of a new 2017 theorem.",["sanabria2017symplectic"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
