add(467,"P000854-P000856","definition","Projective twistors and the Hermitian null cone",
 r"\(\mathbb C^4\setminus0\to\mathbb CP^3\), a Hermitian form of signature \((2,2)\), positive/negative/null twistors and a seven-dimensional cone \(N\).",
 r"Let \(T=\mathbb C^4\) have nondegenerate Hermitian form \(h\) of signature \((2,2)\). A projective twistor is a complex line \([z]\in\mathbb P(T)\), whose positive, negative or null type is well defined because \(h(cz,cz)=|c|^2h(z,z)\). The nonzero null cone is a smooth real seven-manifold; its projectivization has real dimension five.",
 r"The origin is a singular point of the cone and is excluded in the dimension statement. The dual projective space describes the dual twistor construction, not an extra choice of four-dimensional spacetime coordinates.",
 r"This is standard preexisting twistor geometry, checked in the classical-geometry sections of \citet[Secs.~3.1 and~3.3.2]{woit2021euclidean}. No claim from that paper's unification proposal is used.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(467,"P000855","construction","The Grassmannian and its frame bundle",
 r"Projective lines in \(\mathbb CP^3\) form \(G_{4,2}^{\mathbb C}\); ordered two-frames form a principal bundle \(E\to G\).",
 r"The Grassmannian \(G=\operatorname{Gr}(2,T)\) has complex dimension four. The space \(E\) of ordered linearly independent pairs in \(T\) maps to \(G\) by span, with free transitive right \(GL_2(\mathbb C)\) action on each fibre. It is the frame bundle of the tautological rank-two bundle.",
 r"The two-frames are bases of variable two-planes; they need not be orthonormal for the indefinite Hermitian form.",
 r"Classical Grassmannian geometry; the twistor incidence interpretation is checked in \citet[Sec.~3.3]{woit2021euclidean}. This continues 1978-455.1.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="correct")
add(467,"P000855-P000856","construction","The graph coordinate chart",
 r"After choosing transverse two-planes \(L,L'\), P000855 says any other is a graph; P000856 restores \(\widetilde L\cap L'=0\).",
 r"For \(T=L\oplus L'\), the open set \(G_0=\{W:W\cap L'=0\}\) is identified with \(\operatorname{Hom}(L,L')\) by \(U\mapsto\operatorname{graph}U\). Every such graph has basis \(e_A+\sum_{B'}u_{AB'}e^{B'}\), with row/column conventions fixed.",
 r"The transversality condition excludes a Schubert divisor. The later page supplies it explicitly, so the earlier universal wording is corrected internally.",
 r"Elementary Grassmannian coordinates, checked directly by projection \(W\to L\); the same chart construction is used in \citet[Sec.~3.3]{woit2021euclidean}.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(467,"P000855-P000856","problem","Complementary isotropic planes and the GL2 subgroup",
 r"Prove the existence of transverse isotropic \(L,L'\), then prove that \(U(2,2)\) contains a copy of \(GL_2(\mathbb C)\).",
 r"In a Hermitian space of signature \((2,2)\), choose positive orthonormal vectors \(p_i\) and negative orthonormal vectors \(q_i\). Their sums and suitable imaginary multiples of their differences give complementary isotropic two-planes with form matrix \(H=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&iI\\-iI&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)\). Then \(g\mapsto\operatorname{diag}(g,(g^*)^{-1})\) is an injective homomorphism into \(U(H)\).",
 r"Direct block multiplication gives \(G^*HG=H\). The mixed pairings determine the conjugate inverse transformation of the second basis. Their reversed pairings are \(-i\), so the phrase all other scalar products zero means only the independent pairings.",
 r"The exercise is solved by elementary Witt decomposition and the displayed block check; it belongs to preexisting indefinite-unitary linear algebra \citep[Sec.~3.3.2]{woit2021euclidean}.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(467,"P000856-P000857","construction","Tube domains, real boundary and boundary strata",
 r"\(S_\pm\) are positive/negative two-planes, with \((U-U^*)/i\) definite; \(M_0=\{U=U^*\}\) is their common skeleton.",
 r"In a compatible graph convention, positivity of the restricted Hermitian form is equivalent to positive definiteness of \(\operatorname{Im}U=(U-U^*)/(2i)\); negativity is analogous. The real chart of maximal isotropic planes is \(\operatorname{Herm}_2(\mathbb C)\), of real dimension four. The finite boundary of the positive tube has \(\operatorname{Im}U\ge0\) singular, including rank-one and rank-zero strata.",
 r"The Hermitian locus is the distinguished real boundary, not the entire topological boundary. Positive and negative planes are automatically transverse to the fixed isotropic complement; maximal isotropic planes need not be.",
 r"Classical matrix-domain geometry underlying the Minkowski real form \citep[Sec.~3.3.2]{woit2021euclidean}; the definiteness criterion follows by restricting \(H\) to a graph.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(467,"P000857","theorem","Transitivity and conformal compactification",
 r"\(U(2,2)\) preserves \(S_+,S_-,M\) and acts transitively on each; \(M\) is the conformal compactification of Minkowski space.",
 r"The group \(U(2,2)\) acts transitively on maximal positive, maximal negative and maximal isotropic complex two-planes. The last space is compact: in the positive/negative decomposition, each maximal isotropic plane is the graph of a unitary map, giving \(M\simeq U(2)\). Its dense Hermitian chart carries the Minkowski conformal class.",
 r"Transitivity follows by extending adapted bases to Hermitian isometries. \(U(2)\) is \((S^1\times SU(2))/\{\pm1\}\), so the compactification has the usual finite-cover convention; no unqualified \(S^1\times S^3\) identification is imposed.",
 r"The compactification is preexisting twistor theory and is checked in \citet[Sec.~3.3.2]{woit2021euclidean}.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(467,"P000857","construction","Fractional-linear action on matrix coordinates",
 r"For \(g=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}A&B\\C&D\end{smallmatrix}\right)\), the action is \(U\mapsto(AU+B)(CU+D)^{-1}\).",
 r"If a plane is represented by columns \(\binom{U}{I}\), block multiplication and renormalization give exactly \(g\cdot U=(AU+B)(CU+D)^{-1}\), on the open set where \(CU+D\) is invertible. Using columns \(\binom{I}{U}\) instead gives \((C+DU)(A+BU)^{-1}\).",
 r"The earlier graph-basis notation appears to use the opposite ordering. Both formulas express the same Grassmannian action after a consistent chart convention; the rational map is not defined on the whole affine chart for every \(g\).",
 r"Elementary projective/Grassmannian linear algebra, directly checked by block multiplication; compare \citet[Sec.~3.3.2]{woit2021euclidean}.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses")
add(467,"P000857","claim","The affine chart stabilizer includes dilations",
 r"The subgroup preserving \(G_0\), acting by \(U\mapsto aUa^*+b\), \(a\in GL_2(\mathbb C)\), \(b=b^*\), is said to be isomorphic to the Poincaré group.",
 r"The displayed affine action preserves the Minkowski quadratic form up to the constant factor \(|\det a|^2\), because \(\det(a\,dU\,a^*)=|\det a|^2\det(dU)\). It is a similarity group including positive dilations. Restricting \(|\det a|=1\), and quotienting the scalar \(U(1)\) kernel, gives the connected Lorentz part together with translations.",
 r"For example \(a=2I\) sends \(U\) to \(4U\), which is not a Minkowski isometry. The covering relation between \(SL_2(\mathbb C)\) and the proper connected Lorentz group must also be distinguished from isomorphism.",
 r"The classical conformal action is reviewed in \citet[Sec.~3.3.2]{woit2021euclidean}; the counterexample follows immediately from the notebook formula.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="false as written",match="partial")
add(467,"P000857-P000858","claim","The determinant is the Minkowski quadratic form",
 r"\(\det(dU)\) is the metric; the Pauli matrix uses coordinates \(u_0,u_1,u_3,u_4\), but the following line uses \(u_0,u_1,u_2,u_3\) and ends with a cube.",
 r"For \(U=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x_0-x_1&x_2+ix_3\\x_2-ix_3&x_0+x_1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\), \(\det(dU)=dx_0^2-dx_1^2-dx_2^2-dx_3^2\). This is a quadratic form of signature \((1,3)\), whose polarization defines the Minkowski metric.",
 r"The relabelling of spatial coordinates must be made consistently. The final exponent \(3\), visible in the original PNG, is a faithful error and must be \(2\).",
 r"This is an elementary determinant calculation in the standard spinor realization \citep[Sec.~3.1]{woit2021euclidean}.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="false as written")
add(467,"P000858","theorem","Intersecting twistor lines and the light cone",
 r"All projective lines meeting a fixed line \(\ell_0\) satisfy \(\det(U^0-U)=0\); on the real slice this is the light cone.",
 r"Two graph two-planes \(W_U,W_V\) meet nontrivially if and only if \(\ker(U-V)\ne0\), equivalently \(\det(U-V)=0\). For Hermitian \(U,V\), this means their Minkowski separation is null.",
 r"The statement concerns the graph chart; the Grassmannian incidence condition itself extends globally. The cone includes its vertex \(U=V\), where the intersection dimension jumps from one to two.",
 r"Classical twistor incidence, also present in the earlier seminar occurrence 1978-455.1; checked in \citet[Sec.~3.3.2]{woit2021euclidean} and directly by solving the graph intersection equations.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="correct")
add(467,"P000858","claim","Correcting the point/ray correspondence",
 r"One sentence claims a bijection between points of \(M\) and rays in \(N\); the following line correctly relates light rays on \(M\) to isotropic projective twistors.",
 r"A point of compactified Minkowski space corresponds to a maximal isotropic two-plane in \(T\), hence to a projective line \(\mathbb P^1\) of null twistors. A projective null twistor corresponds to the null geodesic formed by all maximal isotropic two-planes containing that line in \(T\).",
 r"The first claimed bijection is dimensionally impossible: \(M\) has real dimension four, while the projective null-twistor space has dimension five. The incidence relation, with its different fibres, is the correct replacement.",
 r"The standard correspondence is explicit in \citet[Sec.~3.3.2]{woit2021euclidean}. The contradiction is already visible between the two adjacent notebook sentences.",["woit2021euclidean"],literal="false as written")
